--TEST--
`Core\Html::parse` recovers by the WHATWG algorithm rather than by guessing: misnested formatting is reconstructed and content stray inside a table is fostered out of it, at the exact place every browser puts it — `rule:core-classes/html-parsing`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// One line per node, indented by depth, so this case pins *placement* — the
// half of the algorithm a hand-written parser cannot agree with, since there is
// nothing to agree with except the specification.
class Tree {
    public static function show(Core\Xml\Node $node, string $indent): void {
        foreach ($node->children() as Core\Xml\Node $child) {
            if ($child->kind() == Core\Xml\NodeKind::Element) {
                echo $indent, $child->name(), "\n";
            }
            if ($child->kind() == Core\Xml\NodeKind::Text) {
                echo $indent, "\"", $child->text(), "\"\n";
            }
            Tree::show($child, $indent . "  ");
        }
    }

    // The `body` of a parsed document, which is where every case below puts its
    // content — reached rather than assumed, so a document that placed it
    // somewhere else prints nothing and fails loudly.
    public static function body(string $document): Core\Xml\Node {
        foreach (Core\Html::parse($document)->children() as Core\Xml\Node $html) {
            foreach ($html->children() as Core\Xml\Node $child) {
                if ($child->name() == "body") {
                    return $child;
                }
            }
        }
        throw new LogicError("a parsed document has a body");
    }
}

// The adoption agency algorithm: `<b>` is still open when `</p>` closes the
// paragraph, so the formatting is *reconstructed* outside it. A parser that
// simply closed everything would put "after" at the top of the body, and one
// that ignored the mismatch would leave it inside the paragraph. Neither is
// what a browser shows.
echo "-- misnested formatting\n";
Tree::show(Tree::body("<p><b>bold<i>both</p>after"), "");

// Foster parenting: text and elements that cannot live in a table are moved to
// just before it, not dropped and not left inside. `tbody` is implied on the
// way, which is the same algorithm supplying a tag the document omitted.
echo "-- fostered out of a table\n";
Tree::show(Tree::body("<table>stray<tr><td>cell"), "");

// The other half of the same rule, and the half a "close everything on a block
// tag" parser gets wrong: a block start tag closes an open paragraph and
// nothing else, so a `div` written inside open formatting *nests inside it* and
// the text after the block is still bold. Reconstruction happens where an
// element was closed out of order, not wherever a block begins.
echo "-- a block inside open formatting\n";
Tree::show(Tree::body("<b>one<div>two</div>three"), "");

// Character data around markup is one text node per run, not one per buffer the
// tokenizer happened to fill — `TreeSink`'s adjacent-text contract, which is
// what makes the shape of a tree independent of how the bytes arrived.
echo "-- adjacent text is one node\n";
Tree::show(Tree::body("a&amp;b<br>c&#100;d"), "");
--EXPECT--
-- misnested formatting
p
  b
    "bold"
    i
      "both"
b
  i
    "after"
-- fostered out of a table
"stray"
table
  tbody
    tr
      td
        "cell"
-- a block inside open formatting
b
  "one"
  div
    "two"
  "three"
-- adjacent text is one node
"a&b"
br
"cdd"
